700 chips.  This is a significant deficit I need to address. Each hand starts with 20,000 chips, and the goal is to have more chips than my opponent by the end of hand 20, suggesting cumulative chip wins determine the winner.  So, I need to make some moves.\n\nThis is a Heads-Up (1v1) situation, which changes things a bit. QTo is a decent hand, roughly in the top 30-35%, but in this position, the SB is considered out-of-position (OOP) pre-flop.  I have 19,950 chips, meaning I\'ve already posted the Small Blind of 50. My opponent, the Big Blind (BB), has 19,900 and 100 in the pot.  I\'m first to act.\n\nThe valid actions are listed as: [-2, -1, 0, 200...], where -2 is all-in, -1 is fold, and 0 is check. The system also gives a raise action, `200`.\n\nThe "0" action initially throws me off. It usually means check or call. It looks like "0" here represents the "call" action for my small blind. "0 (Check)" doesn\'t make sense preflop SB as I would be "calling" the bet of the blinds. I will act like "0" is call.\n\nSo, I need to make a decision. Folding is out of the question because this is a decent hand in this position. Since I am already down, and there are only 3 hands left, my goal is to maximize the chips won per hand to overcome the massive chip deficit. Checking could be valid, but I\'ll likely need to "act" in this scenario if I am to win.\n\nThere are 2 main strategies. Call or Raise:\n*   Calling would mean calling the extra 50, to see the flop for cheap and potentially hit a good hand.\n*   Raising could put pressure on the BB.\n\nNow here\'s the dilemma. Given I am down substantially, I need to start aggressively building pots. It\'s time to test them and start a big pot. QTo is strong enough to play more aggressively by raising or calling. We can potentially steal the blinds, but with 200bb deep stacks, limping/calling is a solid strategy to keep the pot smaller. However, I want to win chips.\n\nI want to win the hand and erase the deficit. In the long run, winning small pots will not make a difference. QTo is decent enough to be aggressive so it\'s best to raise. Raising to 200, which is double the BB, feels like the right play. It applies pressure, allows me to take the pot immediately if they fold and gives initiative in postflop play. However, I can always fold if re-raised, as a QTo is too weak to play a big pot OOP. However, given my need to erase the chip deficit, it makes sense to go with the raise.\n\nTherefore, given the need to generate chips and test the opponent, I\'m going with action **200 (raise the bet)**.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: Your hand is Queen of Clubs and 10 of Spades (QTo). In heads-up play, this is a strong starting hand (roughly top 30-35%). You are in the Small Blind position. Since the stacks reset to 20,000 every hand and you are deep-stacked (200 Big Blinds), you have maneuverability. However, you are significantly behind in the cumulative score (-19,700) with only 3 hands remaining. To have any chance of catching up, you need to play aggressively to build larger pots, rather than keeping the pot small with a limp. Raising applies pressure, takes the initiative, and starts building a pot that you hope to win either pre-flop or by hitting the board.\n\nReason: QTo is strong enough to open for a raise. Raising to 200 (min-raise) is a standard play to steal the blind or take initiative. Given the large cumulative deficit, playing for a larger pot is strategically necessary.\n\nAnswer: 200'}
